Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 01:51:08 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 18/40] Dispersive line hooks: the orders of a position back to
 back, four positions at a time
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A conducting sheet is a two-order ADE on one set of positions, already in x order; the line hooks of the
fused sweep ran each order over all positions, one position and one float at a time. When every order with
voltage (current) ADE has the same positions in x order, the pre-update (Engine_Ext_LorentzMaterial::
PreMerged) and the apply (Engine_Ext_Dispersive::ApplyMerged) now run these orders back to back per
position, four positions at a time as f4vectors (field values gathered and scattered lane by lane), with
the same operations per float in the same order: the ADE states of different orders are independent, and
the apply still subtracts order 0 before order 1 from each field value. OPENEMS_ADE_MERGED=0: one order
after the other, as before; verbose > 1 prints the merged orders.

The merged apply gathers and scatters four positions at a time, so a position listed twice in one group of
four would lose one of its updates: MergeOrders() also requires every position to appear once (the builders
list each cell once, so this never fails today). The check follows the strict-order check of upstream PR #245
by Roel Budé (unsettledroell404); the rest of that PR (the unfused hooks split over the threads) is not
needed here, the line hooks of the fused sweep already run on every thread.

Verified bit-identical against the template hooks of the single-threaded engine on boxes with conducting
sheets, Drude/Lorentz blocks (with and without the Lorentz pole) and both, at 1, 3 and 4 threads, and on
the six benchmark models against stock (bench/check.sh). Paired A/B on the GCPW line (6624 sheet
positions, 8 rounds): x1.041 at 4 threads, x1.031 at 8; no effect on the models without sheets.

